    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d429">

                <pb facs="0450" n="446" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg446"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.943 Manchuria/52</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hamilton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-23">February 23, 1938</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The new Counselor of the Belgian Embassy, Baron Herve de Gruben, called and left
                    with me the attached paper<anchor n="90" xml:id="d429-f1"/> as an informal
                    memorandum indicative of the views of the Belgian Government toward the
                    declaration of the “Manchukuo” government in regard to the abolition of
                    extraterritoriality in Manchuria. Baron de Gruben referred to the fact that some
                    time ago we had furnished the Belgian Ambassador information in regard to the
                    attitude of this Government toward the question of extraterritorial jurisdiction
                    in “Manchukuo”.<anchor n="91" xml:id="d429-f2"/> Baron de Gruben said that the
                    Belgian Government had made known its views in the matter to the “Manchukuo”
                    authorities. In reply to inquiry, he said that the Belgian Government had made
                    no approach in the matter at Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed>M[<hi rendition="#small-caps">axwell</hi>] M. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">amilton</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
